EXEC_SUMMARY = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "Executive Summary"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The two largest inventories of legacy high-level radioactive tank waste in the United States sit at the Savannah River Site (SRS) in South Carolina and the Hanford Site in Washington. This paper compares what remains in the tanks as of the most recent public inventories: 33.2 million gallons and 191 megacuries across 51 SRS tanks (as of September 30, 2025), and 53.0 million gallons and 122.5 megacuries across 177 Hanford tanks (Best-Basis Inventory snapshots per tank, mostly effective between 2022 and 2026, decayed predominantly to January 1, 2022). SRS holds 63 percent of Hanford's volume but 156 percent of its reported activity, a 2.5-fold difference in activity density driven by salt waste chemistry: SRS supernate retains cesium-137 in solution at high concentration, while Hanford's inventory is older and its activity is more evenly split between strontium-90 in sludge and cesium-137 in saltcake and supernatant."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The two inventories are not equivalent bases. Hanford publishes a per-tank Best-Basis Inventory (BBI) with current radionuclide and chemical estimates by waste phase; SRS does not publish a per-tank current inventory, and the public per-tank data are closure residuals: conservative performance-assessment bases decayed to 2032, which represent what regulators expect to remain after bulk waste removal and cleaning. To compare the sites on a like-for-like current basis, we supplement SRS closure data with farm-level current phase totals and measured sludge-batch and salt-batch qualification data from Savannah River National Laboratory (SRNL). This mixed basis is inherent to any cross-site comparison and is quantified throughout."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The chemical contrast is the second major finding. Total non-water chemical inventory at Hanford is roughly 150,200 metric tons, dominated by sodium nitrate and nitrite and about 46,400 t of sodium. The SRS closure-residual chemical matrix (27 analytes across 51 tanks, residual-solids basis) totals about 299 t and is dominated by iron (94 t), aluminum (57 t), manganese (18 t), and 19 t of mercury. SRS chemistry reflects reprocessing with mercury-catalyzed digestion and a nitrate/nitrite working fluid; Hanford chemistry reflects bismuth-phosphate and REDOX/plutonium-uranium-extraction (PUREX) process chemistries with large sodium additions for alkaline stabilization. The SRS salt system carries about 19 t of mercury that is absent from Hanford's BBI; conversely Hanford carries about 566 t of chromium in its inventory, ten times SRS's residual-solids value, a legacy of sodium dichromate coolant additions to some single-shell tanks."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "We compiled both inventories into a single SQLite database (56,700 rows across nine tables) with per-tank, per-nuclide, and per-chemical resolution, public-source provenance on every row, and released the parsed data, parse scripts, and both an interactive Streamlit dashboard and a static HTML dashboard. The database supports direct statistical comparison of the two sites and is positioned for machine-learning work on tank-waste characterization. The comparison suggests a simple conclusion for practitioners: Hanford inventory is big, cold, and chemically simple; SRS inventory is smaller, hotter per gallon, and chemically richer, with mercury and aluminum as distinguishing signatures. Any quantitative risk or mission modeling that mixes the two sites must respect the closure-versus-current basis difference and the different decay dates, or results will be wrong by factors, not percentages."},
]

INTRO = [
    {"t": "h1", "text": "1. Introduction"},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "1.1 Two sites, two inventory problems"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Defense reprocessing waste from five decades of weapons-material production is stored in large underground tanks at SRS and Hanford. The SRS F- and H-Area Tank Farms hold 51 carbon-steel tanks in four generations of design (Types I through IV), ranging from 750,000 to 1.3 million gallons, total capacity 58.6 million gallons, 8 of them operationally closed. Hanford's tank farms group 177 tanks across six farm complexes (A through U), single-shell tanks (SSTs, 149) and double-shell tanks (DSTs, 28), with capacities between 55,000 and 1.3 million gallons. Both sites must empty the tanks, convert the waste to glass or grouted waste forms, and close the farms to regulatory standards set by state agreements and, for closure, Section 3116 of the Ronald W. Reagan National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2005."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The scale of the two problems differs by mission design, not by physics alone. SRS separates sludge (which holds most actinides and strontium) for vitrification at the Defense Waste Processing Facility (DWPF) and decontaminates salt supernate by cesium/strontium/actinide removal at the Salt Waste Processing Facility (SWPF), grouting the decontaminated effluent at Saltstone. Hanford's Direct-Feed Low-Activity Waste (DFLAW) approach likewise splits the tank waste: high-level solids go to the Waste Treatment Plant (WTP), and low-activity liquids are vitrified in the LAW (low-activity waste) facility. The sites differ in how much waste remains to be processed and how much of the curie inventory each phase carries, which is exactly the kind of question a unified dataset can answer."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "1.2 The Best-Basis Inventory and the SRS gap"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Hanford's BBI is the site's official per-tank inventory: a point-in-time estimate of each tank's radionuclides and chemicals by waste phase, assembled by PNNL from process-knowledge models and sample data, published in the Tank Waste Information Navigator System (TWINS) and summarized in the Waste Information Data System (WIDS, HNF-EP-0182). The BBI is the working inventory for mission planning, safety basis, and regulatory reporting, and is available through the PNNL PHOENIX API for programmatic access. It carries 50 radionuclides and 140 chemicals per tank, by phase, with unit " + '"' + "Ci" + '"' + " and " + '"' + "kg" + '"' + " and data-quality qualifiers, and an effective date per tank snapshot."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "SRS has no public equivalent. Savannah River tracks current tank-by-tank composition internally in the Waste Characterization System (WCS), which is not published per-tank. What SRS publishes at per-tank granularity is closure residual inventories: the performance-assessment (PA) and special-analysis (SA) tables that assign each tank a conservative residual source term, decayed to a 2032 closure horizon, after waste removal and chemical cleaning. These tables are the basis for the Section 3116 determinations and NRC consultations, and they are the only per-tank multi-analyte SRS inventories in the public record. Current SRS composition is available only at farm level (phase volumes and curies in the Liquid Waste System Plan, LWSP) and in SRNL qualification reports for processing batches (sludge batches and salt batches). The comparison in this paper therefore rests on three distinct data types: Hanford BBI per-tank current inventories, SRS per-tank closure residuals, and SRS batch qualification measurements. Section 3 describes how each was assembled and normalized."},
    {"t": "h2", "text": "1.3 Why a unified dataset"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Tank-waste data science has matured at Hanford because the BBI exists in machine-readable form and because the site publishes unified retrieval plans and mission models against it. SRS data exists but is scattered across PA/SA tables and SRNL reports in PDF form. Assembling the SRS data into a schema mirroring the BBI (tank × phase × analyte, long format) does two things. It makes the SRS per-tank data usable for the same class of analysis, and it exposes precisely where the sites' public data models differ, which is itself a finding about the state of public tank-waste information. The companion database released with this paper (srs_tank_waste.db) normalizes 4,817 SRS radionuclide records, 1,987 SRS chemical records, 607 measured sample rows, and 46,600 Hanford BBI rows under one schema with per-row provenance."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents the comparative results: total volumes and activities, phase splits, per-farm and per-tank distributions, radionuclide and chemical composition, and measured current-waste data for SRS batches. Section 3 details the data sources and the assembly methodology, including the parsing pipeline and the normalization decisions. Section 4 discusses the basis-difference problem and its implications for cross-site work. Section 5 draws conclusions. All values in this paper are computed from the companion database unless otherwise noted."},
]
